    "q-269378": {
      "dom_id": "step-q269378",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$",
      "worked_solution": "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-40 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
