    "q-269383": {
      "dom_id": "step-q269383",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$",
      "worked_solution": "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 30 \\\\ -3 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 45\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-269383-a",
