    "q-269386": {
      "dom_id": "step-q269386",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]?$",
      "worked_solution": "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}2{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y{x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}-18 & 42 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & 84 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 14\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-269386-a",
